# Setup and authentication

## Local installation

The provider is not yet published to the Registry. From the repository root, build it with the Go version in `go.mod`:

```shell
go build -o terraform-provider-twenty .
```

Add a development override to your [Terraform CLI configuration](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/config/config-file), usually `~/.terraformrc`. Use the absolute directory containing the binary:

```hcl
provider_installation {
  dev_overrides {
    "glitchedmob/twenty" = "/absolute/path/to/terraform-provider-twenty"
  }
  direct {}
}
```

For the standalone provider examples, run `terraform plan` directly. `terraform init` still tries to install the unpublished provider from the Registry.

## Automation account

Use an existing account with verified email, password sign-in, and membership in the intended active workspace. Provision it before configuring the provider: Twenty login can consume a pending invitation and join the user, even before returning an email-verification error.

Keep this account, its roles, and an independent full-settings recovery administrator outside Terraform-managed resources. The provider's guards do not verify that the recovery administrator can log in.

API keys, SSO-only sign-in, interactive MFA, and CAPTCHA are unsupported. Do not weaken server security controls to use the provider.

## Credentials

Inject `TWENTY_ENDPOINT`, `TWENTY_EMAIL`, and `TWENTY_PASSWORD` into the Terraform process, or use the corresponding [provider attributes](../index.md#schema). Never commit credentials or put secrets in shell history. For explicit inputs, Terraform 1.10+ supports [ephemeral variables](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/values/variables#ephemeral); `sensitive` alone redacts output but does not prevent persistence in saved plans.

Use the HTTPS instance base URL, without `/metadata` or another path. Redirects are rejected. Local HTTP requires `allow_insecure_http = true` and a `localhost` or literal loopback address; certificate verification is never disabled.

Each provider configuration starts a new session. Access and rotating refresh tokens stay in process memory and are not stored in Terraform state. If renewal fails, start a new Terraform run.

## Permissions

| Operation | Required settings permissions |
| --- | --- |
| Read current workspace | None |
| Read, create, or update roles | `ROLES` |
| Delete roles | `ROLES`, `APPLICATIONS`, `WORKSPACE_MEMBERS` |
| Manage or import memberships | `ROLES`, `WORKSPACE_MEMBERS` |

Role mutations also need visibility of current workspace members. A role with `can_update_all_settings = true` covers all settings permissions; object-record grants do not.
